  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A round of showers and thunderstorms this\nevening into the overnight period. \n\nAnother short wave moving across the Great Lakes and upper Ohio\nRiver Valley region this afternoon will dive SE towards MD/VA\nlater today. It will create the opportunity for more scattered \nshowers and tstms across (mostly) southern and western parts of \nthe CWA. We have preserved the 30%-40% pops for the evening and \nearly overnight periods. While only the SW corner of our Delmarva\nareas are in Day 1 SPC outlook, strong winds gusts are possible\nwith any tstm. The activity diminishes after midnight-2AM and \nskies will become partly cloudy following it. Patchy fog across \nrural areas that receive any rain. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and comfortable weather expected for \nLabor Day Weekend.\n\nBy Saturday, the trend among model guidance shows the frontal \nboundary south of our region, with Canadian high pressure \nbuilding in across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. This will \nlargely end any shower and thunderstorm potential, however a\nstray shower or two cannot entirely be ruled out. All in all, \nLabor Day Weekend is still shaping up to feature nice weather, \nwith temperatures trending at or slightly below normal and\ncomfortable humidity levels.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will pass through on Thursday\nbringing the potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nBy Tuesday, high pressure will begin to shift offshore welcoming\nthe return of slightly above-normal temperatures and an increase\nin humidity levels. High pressure looks like it will still be \nin control through mid-week, before giving way to a cold front\nthat will be approaching from the north and west Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. This front will be the next best opportunity for\nany showers or thunderstorms around the area. As of now, PoPs\nare in the 20-30% range.",
